{
  "dom_id": "step-q83855",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A rumor is spreading in a school of $2\\,000$ students. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who have heard the rumor is jointly proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the number who have not heard the rumor. If the constant of proportionality is $2.5,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the rumor's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of people who have heard the rumor follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who heard the rumor is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000-P).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
